winewayland, win32u: Publish a GPU child window's new geometry with the window's next flush.

His words (2026-07-30, still live 2026-10-20): "the image of another thumbnail would flicker over the place of another
thumbnail for a split second" (Premiere Pro media bin, hovering over clips). 11.10-146 removed the main cause (a GDI copy
of the bits under the preview, patch 66); this is what was left.

On each hover change Premiere shows its new preview window (WS_CHILD, a D3D swapchain) over the tile, then moves it,
still visible, to a parking spot and hides it ~0.5 ms later. The driver committed every window-management change of a
client subsurface at once (one parent commit per SetWindowPos), so the compositor showed the preview's first frame at
the parking spot until the hide. Leaving that commit out removed it (lab experiment: 0 rogue frames in 4 hover sweeps,
272 parking moves, against 10 on 146).

Wine already publishes a window's GDI content in batches, when the thread goes idle (flush_window_surfaces). Now a
client subsurface's position and crop from window management go out with that same commit, so the compositor sees a
window and its children together, as DWM composes a window's surface and its child visuals in one frame:
- winewayland: wayland_client_surface_attach_rect leaves window-management changes pending (geom_pending) when the
  toplevel publishes window surface contents; a present still commits at once. set_window_surface_contents commits
  pending crops synchronized with the toplevel's commit, so crop and position land together (the 2026-10-13 What's New
  rule: a paused video must not keep a stale crop).
- win32u: after a SetWindowPos that updated on-screen client surfaces, the toplevel's window surface is queued
  (publish_client_geometry -> window_surface_publish_geometry) and marked dirty only when flush_window_surfaces really
  flushes (at idle, or after 50 ms on a thread that never idles). Marking it at once let a flush in the middle of the
  app's own move-and-hide (a DC released while the thread had been idle a moment before) publish the move: 7 rogue
  frames in 4 sweeps with that version.
Gate: NEUTRON_CLIENT_GEOM_BATCH=0 (both modules) restores the per-call commit.

Measured (lab Premiere 2025 on the 11.10-146 package + these two modules, nested desk, KWin ScreenShot2 ~110
captures/s, two 25 s hover sweeps per session, input kept in every sweep):
- with the patch: 0 rogue frames in 6 sweeps (408 parking moves); 146: 10 in 4; NEUTRON_CLIENT_GEOM_BATCH=0: 4 in 2.
- harnesses/clipped-gpu-child (Lightroom Classic's What's New): 9/9 phases, gate on and off. The leave-it-out
  experiment FAILS 4 of them (a stopped video vanishes, a scrolled one spills), so the check tells them apart.
- copybits-gpu-child, rowcascade (plain/nested), caption-settext: unchanged (PASS / 1 / 1 / PASS).
- Premiere's Source monitor playing while the Source/Program divider is dragged 200 px and back: same as 146 (no
  frame off 16:9, none torn, none missing; the picture moved in 62 vs 60 captured frames, largest step 13 vs 18 px).

--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -1047,9 +1047,32 @@
     return TRUE;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON client-geom-batch: window surfaces whose client surfaces' geometry changed (publish_client_geometry in
+ * window.c). Marked dirty only when flush_window_surfaces really flushes -- at idle, or after 50 ms on a thread that
+ * never idles -- so a flush made in the middle of the app's own sequence (a DC released while the thread was idle a
+ * moment ago) does not publish a move the app undoes or hides right after. */
+static struct window_surface *geometry_pending[16];
+static UINT geometry_pending_count;
+
+void window_surface_publish_geometry( struct window_surface *surface )
+{
+    UINT i;
+
+    pthread_mutex_lock( &surfaces_lock );
+    for (i = 0; i < geometry_pending_count; i++) if (geometry_pending[i] == surface) break;
+    if (i == geometry_pending_count && i < ARRAY_SIZE(geometry_pending))
+    {
+        window_surface_add_ref( surface );
+        geometry_pending[geometry_pending_count++] = surface;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock( &surfaces_lock );
+}
+
 void flush_window_surfaces( BOOL idle )
 {
     static DWORD last_idle;
+    struct window_surface *pending[ARRAY_SIZE(geometry_pending)];
+    UINT i, npending = 0;
     DWORD now;
     struct window_surface *surface;
 
@@ -1059,10 +1082,22 @@
     /* if not idle, we only flush if there's evidence that the app never goes idle */
     else if ((int)(now - last_idle) < 50) goto done;
 
+    /* NEUTRON client-geom-batch: one pixel is enough for the flush to commit the window, and the commit carries its
+     * client surfaces' new geometry */
+    for (i = 0; i < geometry_pending_count; i++)
+    {
+        window_surface_lock( geometry_pending[i] );
+        if (IsRectEmpty( &geometry_pending[i]->bounds )) SetRect( &geometry_pending[i]->bounds, 0, 0, 1, 1 );
+        window_surface_unlock( geometry_pending[i] );
+        pending[npending++] = geometry_pending[i];
+    }
+    geometry_pending_count = 0;
+
     LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY( surface, &window_surfaces, struct window_surface, entry )
         window_surface_flush( surface );
 done:
     pthread_mutex_unlock( &surfaces_lock );
+    for (i = 0; i < npending; i++) window_surface_release( pending[i] );
 }
 
